{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1140,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Magnets can be created by running currents\nthrough wires, finding a suitable material"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5660,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that naturally has all the magnetic fields\nof its atoms aligned, or forcing the magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9910,
    "dDurationMs": 1130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fields of atoms to align."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11040,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there's one more kind of magnetism that\nall materials exhibit, even those whose constituent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15920,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atoms aren't magnetic - though it's so weak\nthat the other kinds of magnetism often overwhelm"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20460,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21460,
    "dDurationMs": 3969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Basically, an external magnetic field causes\nthe electrons around atoms in a material to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25429,
    "dDurationMs": 4131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "change course, and their new motion generates\nan opposing magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29560,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This field is pretty weak, but it does cause\nthe material to be repulsed from the magnet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33490,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a little bit - for example, if you hang a\nwooden toothpick in a magnetic field, the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37370,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ends will repel the field and it will end\nup aligning across the magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41310,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a convenient way to remember the name\nof this kind of magnetism - diamagnetism - since"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46340,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"dia\" means across, like the \"diameter\" measured\nacross a circle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50100,
    "dDurationMs": 4139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Diamagnetic materials will repel a magnet,\nand a diamagnetic \"compass\" will point across"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54239,
    "dDurationMs": 3491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the magnetic field - that is, it will orient\neast/west."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57730,
    "dDurationMs": 5009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As weak as it is, diamagnetism is pretty darn\nawesome because it's a repulsive effect: any"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62739,
    "dDurationMs": 3821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diamagnetic material will levitate in a strong\nenough magnetic field!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66560,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like this chunk of graphene, or, since water\nis diamagnetic, this frog."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70680,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In principle, humans could also be levitated\nthis way, though the magnetic fields required"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74420,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be enormous."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75920,
    "dDurationMs": 4129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are also a lot of subtleties we've skated\nover, like the fact that nitrogen is diamagnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80049,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even though as an atom it has unpaired electrons\n- one might think that it *should* be at the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83829,
    "dDurationMs": 1961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very least paramagnetic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85790,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But nitrogen atoms bond to form N2 molecules\nwhich have full outer electron shells and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90190,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are thus only diamagnetic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91930,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, molecular O2, as we've\nseen, still has unpaired electrons, and it's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96520,
    "dDurationMs": 1279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paramagnetic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97799,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You've probably also seen how superconductors\ncan levitate in a magnetic field, which is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101899,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a kind of perfect diamagnetism - not only\ndo the currents in a superconductor create"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106039,
    "dDurationMs": 4381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "opposing magnetic fields, they expel magnetic\nfields from the material entirely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110420,
    "dDurationMs": 16879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the root cause is very very different,\nand that's a journey for another day."
    } ]
  } ]
}
